- [Servo] The exotic Zeppelins
of Led down the Mississippi.

754
00:27:00,556 --> 00:27:04,723
The year is 1952, a very
young Stan Getz at the mic.

755
00:27:06,520 --> 00:27:08,520
The Jackie Gleason show!

756
00:27:12,169 --> 00:27:13,496
- [Mike] We bring you into the hangar

757
00:27:13,496 --> 00:27:15,579
in a relaxed kind of way.

758
00:27:16,946 --> 00:27:20,019
- [Servo] Tell the tower
the music's a little loud.

759
00:27:20,019 --> 00:27:21,196
- The military has so much money,

760
00:27:21,196 --> 00:27:25,029
they actually have Nelson
Riddle in the tower.

761
00:27:26,222 --> 00:27:30,188
- [Servo] (singing) Please
be careful of the intake.

762
00:27:30,188 --> 00:27:33,574
- [Mike] I've had to go potty since Toma.

763
00:27:33,574 --> 00:27:36,491
- Could be better, failed to yield.

764
00:27:37,822 --> 00:27:39,239
- [Mike] F minus.

765
00:27:40,303 --> 00:27:41,636
- [Servo] Oh no.

766
00:27:44,030 --> 00:27:45,953
- Hey, James Carville.

767
00:27:45,953 --> 00:27:48,620
(powering down)

768
00:27:49,748 --> 00:27:51,122
- I see you've been upping the newcomers.

769
00:27:51,122 --> 00:27:52,959
What do they think of the Starfighters?

770
00:27:52,959 --> 00:27:53,900
- Well, I don't know about York,

771
00:27:53,900 --> 00:27:55,546
but Witkowski seems to
have been born in one.

772
00:27:55,546 --> 00:27:56,379
- [Mike] Ooh.

773
00:27:56,379 --> 00:27:58,006
- Oh yeah, with the way
his old man feels about it?

774
00:27:58,006 --> 00:27:59,047
Why what happened?

775
00:27:59,047 --> 00:28:01,008
- Well, after a half an
hour of some familiarization

776
00:28:01,008 --> 00:28:02,525
flying, I gave him the feel of it.

777
00:28:02,525 --> 00:28:03,966
He made me ashamed of the thousand hours

778
00:28:03,966 --> 00:28:05,081
I've logged in this bird.

779
00:28:05,081 --> 00:28:06,515
- What's the beef, Bob?

780
00:28:06,515 --> 00:28:07,699
- Well, nothing Colonel, but frankly,

781
00:28:07,699 --> 00:28:09,497
I never saw a neophyte handle an airplane

782
00:28:09,497 --> 00:28:10,645
the way Witkowski did.

783
00:28:10,645 --> 00:28:11,480
- [Mike] What's a neophyte, Bob?

784
00:28:11,480 --> 00:28:13,013
- Sure, he was a little
sloppy on the controls

785
00:28:13,013 --> 00:28:14,626
at the start, but after he got
used to the characteristics,

786
00:28:14,626 --> 00:28:17,228
he threw the book at it, doing
his best to annihilate space.

787
00:28:17,228 --> 00:28:18,061
- You mean he was showing off?

788
00:28:18,061 --> 00:28:19,769
- Not at all.

789
00:28:19,769 --> 00:28:22,017
No, Witkowski's the start of
a darn good fighter pilot.

790
00:28:22,017 --> 00:28:22,850
- [Servo] Why don't you marry him?

791
00:28:22,850 --> 00:28:24,451
- He was obviously trained right.

792
00:28:24,451 --> 00:28:25,669
But more than that, there's something

793
00:28:25,669 --> 00:28:26,966
bringing this drive out in him.

794
00:28:26,966 --> 00:28:27,799
- Of course.

795
00:28:27,799 --> 00:28:28,632
- The congressman?

796
00:28:28,632 --> 00:28:30,233
- Oh maybe, but do you know flying a plane

797
00:28:30,233 --> 00:28:31,213
is like making love?

798
00:28:31,213 --> 00:28:32,247
- [Mike] Uh, you have to pay?

799
00:28:32,247 --> 00:28:33,517
- Once you've had a little experience,

800
00:28:33,517 --> 00:28:36,412
every operator thinks he's an expert.

801
00:28:36,412 --> 00:28:38,280
- [Servo] (singing) The
marriage to build a sky.

802
00:28:38,280 --> 00:28:40,579
- [Mike] Tell me more about love.

803
00:28:40,579 --> 00:28:43,996
- [Servo] Where is the stupid ladder guy?

804
00:28:45,405 --> 00:28:48,855
- Hey, there's airplanes around here.

805
00:28:48,855 --> 00:28:50,654
- [Mike] Up, up, and away.

806
00:28:50,654 --> 00:28:52,686
- Well, hello there.

807
00:28:52,686 --> 00:28:54,692
- Well, hello there, yourself.

808
00:28:54,692 --> 00:28:55,861
- She's bing-a-ling.

809
00:28:55,861 --> 00:28:57,863
- Picture of contentment
while we hesitated

810
00:28:57,863 --> 00:28:58,864
about interrupting.

811
00:28:58,864 --> 00:28:59,697
- Not at all.

812
00:28:59,697 --> 00:29:01,706
Say fellas, this is my wife, Betty.

813
00:29:01,706 --> 00:29:04,593
Betty, this is John
Witkowski and Bill York.

814
00:29:04,593 --> 00:29:06,104
- [Servo] We call 'em
Pinkidink and Porkboy.

815
00:29:06,104 --> 00:29:08,042
- Come on, have a seat, you
guys aren't going anywhere.

816
00:29:08,042 --> 00:29:08,917
- Sure will.

817
00:29:08,917 --> 00:29:10,510
- [Mike] You don't mind if
we continue to make love.

818
00:29:10,510 --> 00:29:11,722
- Some friends at the club.

819
00:29:11,722 --> 00:29:13,274
- Gene told me he was starting off

820
00:29:13,274 --> 00:29:14,541
with a couple bachelors.

821
00:29:14,541 --> 00:29:16,374
- Oh, footloose and fancy-free,

822
00:29:16,374 --> 00:29:17,763
and we're on the prowl.

823
00:29:17,763 --> 00:29:18,596
(growls)

824
00:29:18,596 --> 00:29:19,915
- Well, we can't really say.

825
00:29:19,915 --> 00:29:22,336
We really haven't started to look.

826
00:29:22,336 --> 00:29:23,689
What are you doing tonight?

827
00:29:23,689 --> 00:29:26,169
- Ha, hey, you really are on the prowl.

828
00:29:26,169 --> 00:29:27,386
- You stay out of this.

829
00:29:27,386 --> 00:29:28,638
- Yes, stay out of this.

830
00:29:28,638 --> 00:29:29,471
- [Servo] Honey.

831
00:29:29,471 --> 00:29:30,372
- Now, which one of you two nice young men

832
00:29:30,372 --> 00:29:31,454
is looking for a date?

833
00:29:31,454 --> 00:29:32,287
- Me.

834
00:29:32,287 --> 00:29:33,120
- Right here.

835
00:29:33,120 --> 00:29:34,269
- Well, not to change the subject,

836
00:29:34,269 --> 00:29:37,155
but just how did it go
out there today, huh?

837
00:29:37,155 --> 00:29:38,612
- Gene, you'll never believe it.

838
00:29:38,612 --> 00:29:40,826
I don't know how to explain
it, you'll never believe it.

839
00:29:40,826 --> 00:29:42,941
That bird really wants to go.

840
00:29:42,941 --> 00:29:43,774
- [Mike] I don't believe it.

841
00:29:43,774 --> 00:29:45,326
- It was even more than
Colonel Hunt said it was.

842
00:29:45,326 --> 00:29:47,791
You know, I actually thought that it was

843
00:29:47,791 --> 00:29:50,064
gonna get away from under
us while we were taking off.

844
00:29:50,064 --> 00:29:50,897
- [Mike] Yeah, great.

845
00:29:50,897 --> 00:29:51,730
Can we order?

846
00:29:51,730 --> 00:29:53,593
- I've never experienced
anything like it before.

847
00:29:53,593 --> 00:29:54,426
Never before.

848
00:29:54,426 --> 00:29:56,104
Remember when we were making the change?

849
00:29:56,104 --> 00:29:56,975
- [Mike] Oh boy.

850
00:29:56,975 --> 00:29:58,441
- Yeah.

851
00:29:58,441 --> 00:30:01,817
- Believe me, this change
is even more terrific.

852
00:30:01,817 --> 00:30:03,815
- Uh, you mean, you're
really sold on it, huh?

853
00:30:03,815 --> 00:30:04,648
- Oh!

854
00:30:04,648 --> 00:30:05,481
- [Mike] No.

855
00:30:05,481 --> 00:30:06,398
- I signed the contract today.

856
00:30:06,398 --> 00:30:08,029
- [Servo] He's a regular Sam Levenson.

857
00:30:08,029 --> 00:30:08,998
- Did you hear what he says?

858
00:30:08,998 --> 00:30:10,712
I mean, about the wonderful
flight you had today?

859
00:30:10,712 --> 00:30:12,760
- Everything he says and
an awful lot more too.

860
00:30:12,760 --> 00:30:13,593
(munching sounds)

861
00:30:13,593 --> 00:30:15,143
Gene leaves off on his
first ride in the morning.

862
00:30:15,143 --> 00:30:16,495
- Yeah, and I can hardly wait.

863
00:30:16,495 --> 00:30:18,577
Say, say look, why don't you guys join us

864
00:30:18,577 --> 00:30:19,870
for dinner tonight, come on.

865
00:30:19,870 --> 00:30:20,936
- Aw, negative, but thanks.

866
00:30:20,936 --> 00:30:22,355
I had a snack a little while earlier.

867
00:30:22,355 --> 00:30:23,616
Right now, all I want to do is

868
00:30:23,616 --> 00:30:25,226
make friends with the bartender.

869
00:30:25,226 --> 00:30:26,626
- [Servo] (laughs) Drink
'till I wet myself.

870
00:30:26,626 --> 00:30:27,892
- Gene, I think I'll pass on that too.

871
00:30:27,892 --> 00:30:28,984
You get me a day, and I'll

872
00:30:28,984 --> 00:30:32,573
take you up on that dinner offering, okay?

873
00:30:32,573 --> 00:30:34,157
- We'll see you, nice meeting you, Betty.

874
00:30:34,157 --> 00:30:35,556
- [Mike] It was a great, great scene.

875
00:30:35,556 --> 00:30:37,008
- You know, John, I never really realized

876
00:30:37,008 --> 00:30:38,324
what a tiger you are.

877
00:30:38,324 --> 00:30:39,357
- Sh, not here.

878
00:30:39,357 --> 00:30:41,902
- Why I'm just as
sociable as the next guy.

879
00:30:41,902 --> 00:30:44,023
- Oh, I mean about on that flight today.

880
00:30:44,023 --> 00:30:45,753
Boy, you were really rocking it around.

881
00:30:45,753 --> 00:30:46,586
- What do you mean?

882
00:30:46,586 --> 00:30:47,419
Stevens gave me the controls

883
00:30:47,419 --> 00:30:49,461
and told me not to break
the airplane, that's all.

884
00:30:49,461 --> 00:30:50,294
- Yeah, I know.

885
00:30:50,294 --> 00:30:51,127
- [Servo] Don't pout.

886
00:30:51,127 --> 00:30:52,415
- You've been holding
out on me, old buddy.

887
00:30:52,415 --> 00:30:53,544
- Oh, it's not the first time

888
00:30:53,544 --> 00:30:55,079
either of us have been in a jet.

889
00:30:55,079 --> 00:30:57,185
- First time on one of
these babies for me.

890
00:30:57,185 --> 00:30:59,080
- [Mike] Oh, that Vodka, mm.

891
00:30:59,080 --> 00:31:01,181
- Oh they're all pretty much alike.

892
00:31:01,181 --> 00:31:03,091
This was just a little easier, that's all.

893
00:31:03,091 --> 00:31:04,140
- Easier?

894
00:31:04,140 --> 00:31:05,028
Ha!

895
00:31:05,028 --> 00:31:07,266
Well, this just wants to get
away from me, that's all.

896
00:31:07,266 --> 00:31:08,099
- [Servo] Just drink.

897
00:31:08,099 --> 00:31:09,633
- Come on, let's get off
the flying kick, huh.

898
00:31:09,633 --> 00:31:11,878
We're gonna be on it every
day for the next six months.

899
00:31:11,878 --> 00:31:13,193
- Day and night.

900
00:31:13,193 --> 00:31:15,363
Hey, I live for this stuff, don't you?

901
00:31:15,363 --> 00:31:17,727
- Sure, Bill, flying's
my first love right now.

902
00:31:17,727 --> 00:31:18,560
- [Mike] After drinking.

903
00:31:18,560 --> 00:31:19,876
- Suppose I weren't a throttle jock,

904
00:31:19,876 --> 00:31:22,791
maybe I'd be on a navy
underwater demolition team

905
00:31:22,791 --> 00:31:25,255
or some other wild outfit, and
that would be my first love.

906
00:31:25,255 --> 00:31:26,088
- [Mike] Not decoupage?

907
00:31:26,088 --> 00:31:28,853
- You mean to say you didn't
want in this mans Air Force?

908
00:31:28,853 --> 00:31:29,686
- When I was in college.

909
00:31:29,686 --> 00:31:30,519
- [Mike] Drink.

910
00:31:30,519 --> 00:31:31,729
My dad wanted me to get in the ROTC.

911
00:31:31,729 --> 00:31:32,562
- [Servo] Oh boy.

912
00:31:32,562 --> 00:31:33,395
- [Mike] Liquor.

913
00:31:33,395 --> 00:31:35,128
- My school just happened to
have an Air Force ROTC unit.

914
00:31:35,128 --> 00:31:35,961
- [Crow] Booze.

915
00:31:35,961 --> 00:31:37,663
- Then when I graduated,
he wanted me to get

916
00:31:37,663 --> 00:31:39,331
in the actual flying end of it,

917
00:31:39,331 --> 00:31:40,164
so I'm here, and that's great.

918
00:31:40,164 --> 00:31:40,997
- [Servo] Gin.

919
00:31:40,997 --> 00:31:41,936
- But I wasn't one of
these kids that used to

920
00:31:41,936 --> 00:31:43,685
hang around airports
just to be near pilots

921
00:31:43,685 --> 00:31:45,287
and planes and hangar talk.

922
00:31:45,287 --> 00:31:46,120
- [Mike] Okay, okay.

923
00:31:46,120 --> 00:31:47,116
- Really love it.

924
00:31:47,116 --> 00:31:49,137
I wouldn't trade it for anything, but.

925
00:31:49,137 --> 00:31:49,970
(burp)

926
00:31:49,970 --> 00:31:51,964
I don't see why we have to
breathe it day and night.

927
00:31:51,964 --> 00:31:53,403
- Geez, I started a friendly conversation,

928
00:31:53,403 --> 00:31:54,593
and he takes the floor.

929
00:31:54,593 --> 00:31:57,199
- Okay, sport, here's to
some really good flying.

930
00:31:57,199 --> 00:31:58,377
- I'll drink to that.

931
00:31:58,377 --> 00:32:01,467
- 17-and-a-half scotches later.

932
00:32:01,467 --> 00:32:04,932
- [Mike] (muffled) to realize
I had a aptitude for flying.

933
00:32:04,932 --> 00:32:06,848
'Course I'm as social as the next guy,

934
00:32:06,848 --> 00:32:09,098
have I told you that, over?

935
00:32:10,028 --> 00:32:14,001
- [Servo] Playing tennis,
flying all with your new hair.

936
00:32:14,001 --> 00:32:18,112
- Cordite four, join the
formation, we like you.

937
00:32:18,112 --> 00:32:21,612
- [Mike] The dentist will see you shortly.

938
00:32:22,924 --> 00:32:25,146
- Uh, anyone seen Francis Gary?

939
00:32:25,146 --> 00:32:27,736
Where'd he get off to, that nut?

940
00:32:27,736 --> 00:32:29,226
- [Mike] You know, we can
make fun of these guys,

941
00:32:29,226 --> 00:32:31,441
but Saddam Hussein ain't laughin' huh.

942
00:32:31,441 --> 00:32:32,274
(laughter)

943
00:32:32,274 --> 00:32:34,092
- [Servo] Mike, shall we?

944
00:32:34,092 --> 00:32:36,015
- [Mike] Oh, I'd love to, thank you.

945
00:32:36,015 --> 00:32:39,598
- (humming) The afternoon.

946
00:32:41,795 --> 00:32:43,366
- [Mike] You're as light as air.

947
00:32:43,366 --> 00:32:47,533
- [Servo] Oh, that's lovely,
where did you learn that step?

948
00:32:50,229 --> 00:32:51,062
- [Mike] Thank you.

949
00:32:51,062 --> 00:32:54,102
- [Servo] Oh, I am tired, thank you.

950
00:32:54,102 --> 00:32:58,267
- Here, the Air Force escorts
in anew new to a golf game.

951
00:32:58,267 --> 00:33:00,244
- [Mike] Yes, we've
traded quality education

952
00:33:00,244 --> 00:33:03,161
of a million kids for these planes.

953
00:33:04,661 --> 00:33:06,386
- [Mike] Ah, lovely, lovely.

954
00:33:06,386 --> 00:33:07,969
- [Crow] Very nice.

955
00:33:07,969 --> 00:33:09,185
- [Servo] Ooh, colonel, do laugh.

956
00:33:09,185 --> 00:33:11,779
- Well, gentlemen, now that
you've soloed in Starfighter,

957
00:33:11,779 --> 00:33:13,097
you're entitled to this patch.

958
00:33:13,097 --> 00:33:14,155
- [Mike] Oh great, a patch.

959
00:33:14,155 --> 00:33:15,703
- I'm sure you'll discover
that the next training phase

960
00:33:15,703 --> 00:33:18,217
is more exciting and much more demanding.

961
00:33:18,217 --> 00:33:19,667
Eventually, we'll be depending on you to

962
00:33:19,667 --> 00:33:21,534
perform a variety of fine jobs.

963
00:33:21,534 --> 00:33:22,837
For instance, that pilot right there,

964
00:33:22,837 --> 00:33:24,655
he's going up for another high altitude

965
00:33:24,655 --> 00:33:25,838
side winder firing test.

966
00:33:25,838 --> 00:33:30,089
- Nope, just going to clean
out my plane. (chuckles)

967
00:33:30,089 --> 00:33:31,678
- Colonel Hunt, sir, they've
been looking for you.

968
00:33:31,678 --> 00:33:33,137
Telephone call from Washington.

969
00:33:33,137 --> 00:33:34,927
- And I've been looking for you O'Brien.

970
00:33:34,927 --> 00:33:35,760
- [Mike] What's happening?

971
00:33:35,760 --> 00:33:36,672
- General has just been informed

972
00:33:36,672 --> 00:33:38,712
that five technicians
from the Belgian Air Force

973
00:33:38,712 --> 00:33:40,430
will be on the base sometime this evening,

974
00:33:40,430 --> 00:33:42,329
and you are to escort to escort them

975
00:33:42,329 --> 00:33:43,162
during their stay here.

976
00:33:43,162 --> 00:33:45,393
- What, I'm setup for
night flying this week.

977
00:33:45,393 --> 00:33:46,226
(gulp)

978
00:33:46,226 --> 00:33:47,258
- I know, they'll just have to wait

979
00:33:47,258 --> 00:33:48,847
until after they've seen
how we operate here.

980
00:33:48,847 --> 00:33:49,726
- Well, I'll be going up.

981
00:33:49,726 --> 00:33:51,067
- Ah-ah-ah, temper, temper.

982
00:33:51,067 --> 00:33:52,057
- Aw nuts.

983
00:33:52,057 --> 00:33:54,640
(upbeat music)

984
00:33:57,862 --> 00:33:58,904
- Colonel Hunt speaking.

985
00:33:58,904 --> 00:34:00,301
- Good morning, Colonel Hunt.

986
00:34:00,301 --> 00:34:02,043
You're a hard man to find.

987
00:34:02,043 --> 00:34:02,876
- [Mike] Sarah?

988
00:34:02,876 --> 00:34:03,709
- Who's this?

989
00:34:03,709 --> 00:34:04,554
- Well, it's a little difficult to

990
00:34:04,554 --> 00:34:06,070
introduce oneself over the phone.

991
00:34:06,070 --> 00:34:06,903
- I sell Panola.

992
00:34:06,903 --> 00:34:09,316
- I'm John Witkowski, the
father of one of the pilots

993
00:34:09,316 --> 00:34:11,855
who transferred to your
command a couple of weeks ago.

994
00:34:11,855 --> 00:34:12,688
- Oh.

995
00:34:12,688 --> 00:34:14,536
Yes, our Lieutenant Witkowski.

996
00:34:14,536 --> 00:34:15,839
- [Servo] His face springs into action.

997
00:34:15,839 --> 00:34:17,389
- I do know you be reputation,
of course, congressman.

998
00:34:17,389 --> 00:34:19,424
Pleasure to speak with you in person,

999
00:34:19,424 --> 00:34:21,436
but I believe you boy is up on
a training mission right now

1000
00:34:21,436 --> 00:34:22,391
or I'd have him talk to.

1001
00:34:22,391 --> 00:34:25,013
- No, I merely wanted to
introduce myself to you.

1002
00:34:25,013 --> 00:34:25,997
- [Mike] Perhaps dinner or.

1003
00:34:25,997 --> 00:34:27,854
- We have a bit in common, you see.

1004
00:34:27,854 --> 00:34:29,014
- [Mike] My brother, George.

1005
00:34:29,014 --> 00:34:31,094
- You and I were flying
in the ETO together,

1006
00:34:31,094 --> 00:34:32,538
20 years ago.

1007
00:34:32,538 --> 00:34:35,122
- Quite a lot of us
were in the ETO together

1008
00:34:35,122 --> 00:34:36,006
at that time sir.

1009
00:34:36,006 --> 00:34:36,839
- Maybe so.

1010
00:34:36,839 --> 00:34:37,789
- [Servo] They were in BTO?

1011
00:34:37,789 --> 00:34:38,760
- [Mike] (laughs) I don't know.

1012
00:34:38,760 --> 00:34:41,823
- Now tell me, Colonel
Hunt, how's the lad doing?

1013
00:34:41,823 --> 00:34:43,820
- Well, he came in here with
a couple other officers.

1014
00:34:43,820 --> 00:34:44,653
- [Mike] Leathery.

1015
00:34:44,653 --> 00:34:46,080
- He's going through
routine 104 operational

1016
00:34:46,080 --> 00:34:47,165
procedures right now.

1017
00:34:47,165 --> 00:34:48,495
- Getting along all right then, is he?

1018
00:34:48,495 --> 00:34:50,145
- I only met him once, congressman,

1019
00:34:50,145 --> 00:34:52,328
but he seems like a personal boy to me.

1020
00:34:52,328 --> 00:34:53,161
- [Mike] What do you want?

1021
00:34:53,161 --> 00:34:54,246
- He's had no trouble with his fellow men

1022
00:34:54,246 --> 00:34:55,729
or the aircraft he's flying.

1023
00:34:55,729 --> 00:34:57,875
- Uh, I happen to be looking at the

1024
00:34:57,875 --> 00:35:00,433
TAC tally of accidents, Colonel Hunt.

1025
00:35:00,433 --> 00:35:01,809
- [Servo] (whispers) Oh man.

1026
00:35:01,809 --> 00:35:04,259
- I was frankly shocked at what I saw.

1027
00:35:04,259 --> 00:35:05,129
- What do you mean, sir?

1028
00:35:05,129 --> 00:35:05,962
- [Mike] Shocked.

1029
00:35:05,962 --> 00:35:07,275
- Well, to my knowledge, it was

1030
00:35:07,275 --> 00:35:08,832
considerably more than those sustained

1031
00:35:08,832 --> 00:35:10,392
by the heavy bombers in SAC.

1032
00:35:10,392 --> 00:35:11,225
- Congressman.

1033
00:35:11,225 --> 00:35:12,559
- [Servo] 'Kay how much do you want?

1034
00:35:12,559 --> 00:35:14,173
- Did you ever consider
how many individual flights

1035
00:35:14,173 --> 00:35:15,408
the Tactical Air Command puts in the air

1036
00:35:15,408 --> 00:35:17,525
as compared to Strategic Air Command?

1037
00:35:17,525 --> 00:35:20,141
- I am comparing it with
an accident free record,

1038
00:35:20,141 --> 00:35:21,470
which is what it should be.

1039
00:35:21,470 --> 00:35:22,303
- Exactly right.

1040
00:35:22,303 --> 00:35:23,341
- His forehead has been clear-cut.

1041
00:35:23,341 --> 00:35:24,823
- In this day of engineering,

1042
00:35:24,823 --> 00:35:26,372
the equipment is virtually perfect.

1043
00:35:26,372 --> 00:35:27,205
- Here we go.

1044
00:35:27,205 --> 00:35:29,948
- But we still have the
human factor to contend with.

1045
00:35:29,948 --> 00:35:31,159
That's why your boy is here.

1046
00:35:31,159 --> 00:35:31,992
- [Servo] He's human.

1047
00:35:31,992 --> 00:35:33,870
- He'll be supervising
and handling these planes,

1048
00:35:33,870 --> 00:35:35,321
so that they conform in the manner

1049
00:35:35,321 --> 00:35:37,006
that the Air Force specifies.

1050
00:35:37,006 --> 00:35:38,392
- [Mike] I had that coming.

1051
00:35:38,392 --> 00:35:41,012
- How is John doing in
his flying, Colonel Hunt?

1052
00:35:41,012 --> 00:35:42,067
- He's just started,

1053
00:35:42,067 --> 00:35:43,869
and he has months of intensive training

1054
00:35:43,869 --> 00:35:45,827
on all combat procedures ahead of him.

1055
00:35:45,827 --> 00:35:48,494
- [Mike] That was a great scene.

1056
00:35:51,171 --> 00:35:52,004
- Kaboing!

1057
00:35:52,004 --> 00:35:52,933
(rolling tongue)

1058
00:35:52,933 --> 00:35:57,440
- [Servo] It's the new Air
Force goofy bomb from Wham-O.

1059
00:35:57,440 --> 00:35:58,594
- [Mike] Yeah, go ahead and laugh.

1060
00:35:58,594 --> 00:36:00,309
They got a kitty in that bomb.

1061
00:36:00,309 --> 00:36:01,142
- [Servo] Oh.

1062
00:36:01,142 --> 00:36:03,309
(meowing)

1063
00:36:04,442 --> 00:36:06,192
It landed on pig pen!

1064
00:36:09,342 --> 00:36:11,338
- [Servo] Yeah, dig me, pretty much.

1065
00:36:11,338 --> 00:36:13,505
(humming)

1066
00:36:15,989 --> 00:36:17,071
(explosion)

1067
00:36:17,071 --> 00:36:19,429
- Oh, Mrs. Lockhorn cooked dinner again.

1068
00:36:19,429 --> 00:36:22,582
- [Servo] (laughs) Good
to see, I'm out on a bomb

1069
00:36:22,582 --> 00:36:24,540
in run with my baby.

1070
00:36:24,540 --> 00:36:26,287
Yeah, come on, bounce one for me,

1071
00:36:26,287 --> 00:36:27,859
bounce one, yeah.

1072
00:36:27,859 --> 00:36:28,692
- [Mike] Here we go.

1073
00:36:28,692 --> 00:36:30,106
- [Servo] Sit down, junior.

1074
00:36:30,106 --> 00:36:32,790
We're gonna bomb 'em back to the jazz age.

1075
00:36:32,790 --> 00:36:35,040
(scatting)

1076
00:36:37,724 --> 00:36:39,121
- [Mike] Lieutenant Hebe, it says.

1077
00:36:39,121 --> 00:36:42,848
- [Servo] (laughs) Lift here.

1078
00:36:42,848 --> 00:36:46,577
- Yes, that desert environment
is enhanced by bombing.

1079
00:36:46,577 --> 00:36:48,994
Left alone, it is too placid.

1080
00:36:51,466 --> 00:36:53,429
- [Servo] (singing) Kaboom, kaboom.

1081
00:36:53,429 --> 00:36:55,119
- [All] (singing) Yah-dah-dah
dah-dah-dah dah-dah-dah.

1082
00:36:55,119 --> 00:36:57,662
- [Servo] Just sit right back
and enjoy the Vietnam War

1083
00:36:57,662 --> 00:36:59,829
weeknights after Laughlin.

1084
00:37:03,202 --> 00:37:04,386
- [Mike] Yeah, if this was a war,

1085
00:37:04,386 --> 00:37:06,469
we'd be winning it, easy.

1086
00:37:07,634 --> 00:37:09,525
(upbeat big band music)

1087
00:37:09,525 --> 00:37:11,268
- [Servo] Okay, we're gonna
shake things up a bit.

1088
00:37:11,268 --> 00:37:14,851
Pick it up, now, come
on, and move with us.

1089
00:37:16,220 --> 00:37:19,729
- [Mike] (singing) Bombing, yeah!

1090
00:37:19,729 --> 00:37:20,562
- Whoo!

1091
00:37:23,708 --> 00:37:26,554
- [Mike] Okay, ladies choice now.

1092
00:37:26,554 --> 00:37:30,721
- Drop your payloads to the
sounds of farokian taisure.

1093
00:37:33,727 --> 00:37:35,112
- [Servo] Welcome to
the rainbow gathering.

1094
00:37:35,112 --> 00:37:38,266
Peace is possible in our lifetime, ooh.

1095
00:37:38,266 --> 00:37:40,635
(big band swing music)

1096
00:37:40,635 --> 00:37:42,997
(humming)

1097
00:37:42,997 --> 00:37:47,234
- [Mike] God I love my
job, who needs high school?

1098
00:37:47,234 --> 00:37:51,401
- It's a volatile mixture of
popmunitions and cool jazz.

1099
00:37:52,662 --> 00:37:54,241
(humming)

1100
00:37:54,241 --> 00:37:58,074
- [Servo] And Bob came
back the very next day.

1101
00:38:00,313 --> 00:38:01,686
- [Mike] Well boys, in all fairness,

1102
00:38:01,686 --> 00:38:03,102
I should tell you, occasionally,

1103
00:38:03,102 --> 00:38:06,869
the North Vietnamese will fight back.

1104
00:38:06,869 --> 00:38:10,286
- Ah, Christo's latest installa, ah good.

1105
00:38:15,430 --> 00:38:17,004
- [Servo] Well, if we're ever attacked

1106
00:38:17,004 --> 00:38:20,245
by a white rectangle,
we got it under control.

1107
00:38:20,245 --> 00:38:21,494
See?

1108
00:38:21,494 --> 00:38:22,992
- [Mike] Oh, my thermos of napalm.

1109
00:38:22,992 --> 00:38:24,825
Oh, I'll get it later.

1110
00:38:27,532 --> 00:38:31,304
- [Servo] Here, on apocalypse lights.

1111
00:38:31,304 --> 00:38:32,504
- [Mike] Hey, guys, let's not

1112
00:38:32,504 --> 00:38:34,782
blow everything up the first day, okay?

1113
00:38:34,782 --> 00:38:36,865
We got the whole weekend.

1114
00:38:38,081 --> 00:38:41,914
I think the U.S. should
get out of this movie.

1115
00:38:42,763 --> 00:38:43,596
- [Servo] Hope burial ground?

1116
00:38:43,596 --> 00:38:44,429
Oh well.

1117
00:38:46,406 --> 00:38:48,758
- I hope they blow up Blossom.

1118
00:38:48,758 --> 00:38:50,160
- [Servo] heheh.

1119
00:38:50,160 --> 00:38:54,243
Archery is safe and if. (shouts)

1120
00:38:55,147 --> 00:38:57,330
(mimics explosion)

1121
00:38:57,330 --> 00:38:58,892
- You all want to climb in this car?

1122
00:38:58,892 --> 00:38:59,768
- 'scuse me?

1123
00:38:59,768 --> 00:39:00,713
- Hold it.

1124
00:39:00,713 --> 00:39:02,042
I'm the only one that's going to.

1125
00:39:02,042 --> 00:39:05,351
- Sorry I'm late, honey, but
I made a date for one of you.

1126
00:39:05,351 --> 00:39:06,569
Who wants it?

1127
00:39:06,569 --> 00:39:08,509
- What do you mean made a date, for when?

1128
00:39:08,509 --> 00:39:09,391
- For tomorrow night.

1129
00:39:09,391 --> 00:39:10,900
- I can tell you right
now that it's gotta be me

1130
00:39:10,900 --> 00:39:12,596
without any argument from him.

1131
00:39:12,596 --> 00:39:15,557
He goes on a 24 hour duty as
assistant air drone officer,

1132
00:39:15,557 --> 00:39:16,896
and he knows it.

1133
00:39:16,896 --> 00:39:18,919
- My name has been on the
list for three weeks now,

1134
00:39:18,919 --> 00:39:20,880
and there's nothing I
can really do about it,

1135
00:39:20,880 --> 00:39:22,715
and besides, you never
really should send a boy

1136
00:39:22,715 --> 00:39:23,815
to do a man's job.

1137
00:39:23,815 --> 00:39:26,567
(mock laughter)

1138
00:39:26,567 --> 00:39:28,297
- Level with me, Betty, what's she like?

1139
00:39:28,297 --> 00:39:30,344
- Do you realize that I've been

1140
00:39:30,344 --> 00:39:32,437
stationed here over a month now,

1141
00:39:32,437 --> 00:39:34,101
and this is the first
night that I found out

1142
00:39:34,101 --> 00:39:36,043
this lovely creature exists?

1143
00:39:36,043 --> 00:39:38,303
- Oh Mary didn't exist
much before this past week.

1144
00:39:38,303 --> 00:39:40,557
She just moved to the west
coast with her family.

1145
00:39:40,557 --> 00:39:42,045
I met them all out in the upper valley.

1146
00:39:42,045 --> 00:39:43,402
- So I must have looked lonesome.

1147
00:39:43,402 --> 00:39:44,260
- [Servo] Shut up, Iris.

1148
00:39:44,260 --> 00:39:47,035
- Well, we're gonna take
care of that from here on in.

1149
00:39:47,035 --> 00:39:48,168
Is this your first time on an Air Base?

1150
00:39:48,168 --> 00:39:49,001
- Mm-hmm.

1151
00:39:49,001 --> 00:39:51,063
- Never been around a
bunch of pilots before?

1152
00:39:51,063 --> 00:39:52,665
- [Mike] Well this tail hook and all.

1153
00:39:52,665 --> 00:39:54,107
- So, how about that?

1154
00:39:54,107 --> 00:39:55,825
Someone who will listen for a chance.

1155
00:39:55,825 --> 00:39:57,848
- Well, I wish I cold have
listened a while back.

1156
00:39:57,848 --> 00:39:59,806
Then perhaps I wouldn't
be where I am today.

1157
00:39:59,806 --> 00:40:01,684
(goofy mock laughter)

1158
00:40:01,684 --> 00:40:02,884
- Hey Mary, where are you from anyway?

1159
00:40:02,884 --> 00:40:03,803
- Iowa.

1160
00:40:03,803 --> 00:40:05,057
- [Servoe] Oh, so you're stupid.

1161
00:40:05,057 --> 00:40:07,526
- Iowa, Iowa, say, I've heard
of that place before, Gene.

1162
00:40:07,526 --> 00:40:09,308
Don't we have a cross country schedule

1163
00:40:09,308 --> 00:40:10,440
to take us across there?

1164
00:40:10,440 --> 00:40:12,907
- Yeah, but we can bypass it if you like.

1165
00:40:12,907 --> 00:40:14,228
- No kidding, Iowa.

1166
00:40:14,228 --> 00:40:15,609
. Yes, and I bet you're going to ask me

1167
00:40:15,609 --> 00:40:17,628
if my family raised corn back there.

1168
00:40:17,628 --> 00:40:18,461
- [Mike] Yeah.

1169
00:40:18,461 --> 00:40:19,760
- Well, now that you mention it,

1170
00:40:19,760 --> 00:40:20,932
I was going to do that very thing.

1171
00:40:20,932 --> 00:40:21,921
- Well, go ahead and ask me.

1172
00:40:21,921 --> 00:40:23,407
- Okay, I'm asking you.

1173
00:40:23,407 --> 00:40:25,157
- Actually, we did and we didn't.

1174
00:40:25,157 --> 00:40:26,128
- [Mike] Huh?

1175
00:40:26,128 --> 00:40:27,877
- Thanks a lot, you guys,
you brought me a puzzle.

1176
00:40:27,877 --> 00:40:28,710
- No. (laughs)

1177
00:40:28,710 --> 00:40:29,543
- Okay, I'll bite.

1178
00:40:29,543 --> 00:40:31,445
- Dad had an experimental farm back there.

1179
00:40:31,445 --> 00:40:32,884
- And I suppose you were the one

1180
00:40:32,884 --> 00:40:34,423
that held the tape measure every morning

1181
00:40:34,423 --> 00:40:36,784
just to see how much the
stalks grew overnight.

1182
00:40:36,784 --> 00:40:38,649
- Not quite, but for the last two years,

1183
00:40:38,649 --> 00:40:40,982
I was what they call a corn detasseler.

1184
00:40:40,982 --> 00:40:41,815
- [Servo] Wow.

1185
00:40:41,815 --> 00:40:42,648
- What?

1186
00:40:42,648 --> 00:40:43,481
- A corn detasseler.

1187
00:40:43,481 --> 00:40:45,011
- What in blazes is that?

1188
00:40:45,011 --> 00:40:47,095
- Well, that's a person who removes

1189
00:40:47,095 --> 00:40:49,849
pollen bearing tassels
from five rows of corn.

1190
00:40:49,849 --> 00:40:50,682
- [Servo] Check please.

1191
00:40:50,682 --> 00:40:54,313
- And tassels the next
five rows, and so forth.

1192
00:40:54,313 --> 00:40:56,237
- Go on, I'm flabbergasted.

1193
00:40:56,237 --> 00:40:57,070
- [Mike] Me too.

1194
00:40:57,070 --> 00:40:58,715
- Two rows you skip are the male rows,

1195
00:40:58,715 --> 00:41:00,152
and when the tassels dry out,

1196
00:41:00,152 --> 00:41:01,991
the pollen drips over to the female.

1197
00:41:01,991 --> 00:41:04,480
- [Mike] O-kay. (clears throat)

1198
00:41:04,480 --> 00:41:06,834
- That's how we develop
a hybrid seed corn.

1199
00:41:06,834 --> 00:41:08,025
- Can you beat that?

1200
00:41:08,025 --> 00:41:08,858
- [Servo] Yes.

1201
00:41:08,858 --> 00:41:10,612
- I always knew that sex was corny,

1202
00:41:10,612 --> 00:41:12,953
but did you ever think
that corn could be so sexy?

1203
00:41:12,953 --> 00:41:13,786
(mock laughter)

1204
00:41:13,786 --> 00:41:14,732
- Well, now.

1205
00:41:14,732 --> 00:41:15,971
- [Servo] Have you accepted Jesus?

1206
00:41:15,971 --> 00:41:17,689
- I've been at George for
the past three years now,

1207
00:41:17,689 --> 00:41:19,610
and every time I see a pretty new face,

1208
00:41:19,610 --> 00:41:21,555
I have to find out who it belongs to.

1209
00:41:21,555 --> 00:41:23,214
- So, what's your name, solder. (laughs)

1210
00:41:23,214 --> 00:41:24,318
- Oh hi, Betty.

1211
00:41:24,318 --> 00:41:25,151
- Hi Fred.

1212
00:41:25,151 --> 00:41:26,619
- This is Mary Davidson.

1213
00:41:26,619 --> 00:41:28,153
Mary, this is Fred O'Brien.

1214
00:41:28,153 --> 00:41:30,097
He's one of the flight
commanders in our squadron.

1215
00:41:30,097 --> 00:41:32,232
- Another pilot, won't you join us?

1216
00:41:32,232 --> 00:41:34,747
- Oh by all means, if
only for a little while.

1217
00:41:34,747 --> 00:41:36,443
- Oh, thanks very much,
I don't mind if I do.

1218
00:41:36,443 --> 00:41:38,351
- [Servo] Where's that stupid
Iowa chick, she get here?

1219
00:41:38,351 --> 00:41:39,243
- Well, where were we?

1220
00:41:39,243 --> 00:41:41,786
- You were about to ask
where Mary came from.

1221
00:41:41,786 --> 00:41:43,243
- [Mike] I came from planet Claire.

1222
00:41:43,243 --> 00:41:45,110
- Now tell me, where
did you come from, Mary?

1223
00:41:45,110 --> 00:41:46,626
- Iowa, if you must know.

1224
00:41:46,626 --> 00:41:47,545
- [Mike] Iowa!

1225
00:41:47,545 --> 00:41:49,125
- What'd you do back there?

1226
00:41:49,125 --> 00:41:50,205
- She was a corn detasseler.

1227
00:41:50,205 --> 00:41:51,038
- A what?

1228
00:41:51,038 --> 00:41:52,540
- A corn detasseler.

1229
00:41:52,540 --> 00:41:54,232
- [Mike] Yes, corn detasseler.

1230
00:41:54,232 --> 00:41:56,627
- Well, that's a person who--

1231
00:41:56,627 --> 00:41:58,841
- Hold it, hold it,
once a night is enough.

1232
00:41:58,841 --> 00:42:00,809
- Oh no, I'd like to hear more about this.

1233
00:42:00,809 --> 00:42:03,687
After all, I'm just an
old farm boy myself.

1234
00:42:03,687 --> 00:42:04,923
- Oh you're kidding.

1235
00:42:04,923 --> 00:42:07,002
- No, really, you know, my dad still runs

1236
00:42:07,002 --> 00:42:09,305
a small dairy farm right
outside of Milwaukee.

1237
00:42:09,305 --> 00:42:10,138
- [Mike] Name's Geen.

1238
00:42:10,138 --> 00:42:11,343
- So I got out in time.

1239
00:42:11,343 --> 00:42:13,294
- [Servo] Frankenhooker.

1240
00:42:13,294 --> 00:42:14,354
- [Mike] Wow.

1241
00:42:14,354 --> 00:42:15,187
- Oh.

1242
00:42:15,187 --> 00:42:16,028
Well, dad always said
there were three ways

1243
00:42:16,028 --> 00:42:18,898
a man could bring about
his physical destruction.

1244
00:42:18,898 --> 00:42:20,579
They were by drinking.

1245
00:42:20,579 --> 00:42:22,048
- [Servo] Detasseling.

1246
00:42:22,048 --> 00:42:22,934
- By women,

1247
00:42:22,934 --> 00:42:23,767
by farm.

1248
00:42:24,754 --> 00:42:27,033
- [Crow] Got caught in the power takeoff.

1249
00:42:27,033 --> 00:42:29,678
- My poor father chose the
least exciting of the three.

1250
00:42:29,678 --> 00:42:32,261
(upbeat music)

1251
00:42:35,092 --> 00:42:36,780
